WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.164
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.464 --> 00:00:08.421
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.721 --> 00:00:17.202
Unit fifty: Music, Emotion and Society. In this unit, you will discuss music's social and emotional power with nuance.

4
00:00:18.217 --> 00:00:19.392
musicology.

5
00:00:20.092 --> 00:00:25.923
musicology means the academic study of music, its history and its role in society.

6
00:00:26.223 --> 00:00:31.136
She studied musicology at the Royal University of Fine Arts.

7
00:00:31.836 --> 00:00:35.278
Your turn. Say: musicology.

8
00:00:37.978 --> 00:00:39.130
musicology.

9
00:00:40.117 --> 00:00:41.408
protest song.

10
00:00:42.108 --> 00:00:47.614
protest song means a song that criticises injustice or calls for change.

11
00:00:47.914 --> 00:00:51.307
'Blowin' in the Wind' became a famous protest song.

12
00:00:52.007 --> 00:00:55.472
Your turn. Say: protest song.

13
00:00:58.172 --> 00:00:59.556
protest song.

14
00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:01.702
national anthem.

15
00:01:02.402 --> 00:01:06.573
national anthem means a country's official patriotic song.

16
00:01:06.873 --> 00:01:10.068
Students stand still when the national anthem plays.

17
00:01:10.768 --> 00:01:14.198
Your turn. Say: national anthem.

18
00:01:16.898 --> 00:01:18.108
national anthem.

19
00:01:19.117 --> 00:01:20.629
musical heritage.

20
00:01:21.329 --> 00:01:26.242
musical heritage means the traditional music passed down through generations.

21
00:01:26.542 --> 00:01:32.141
Cambodia's musical heritage nearly vanished in the 1970s.

22
00:01:32.841 --> 00:01:35.888
Your turn. Say: musical heritage.

23
00:01:38.588 --> 00:01:39.879
musical heritage.

24
00:01:40.883 --> 00:01:42.314
emotional resonance.

25
00:01:43.014 --> 00:01:47.939
emotional resonance means the power to produce deep feelings in listeners.

26
00:01:48.239 --> 00:01:52.433
Sinn Sisamouth's songs still have great emotional resonance.

27
00:01:53.133 --> 00:01:56.517
Your turn. Say: emotional resonance.

28
00:01:59.217 --> 00:02:00.787
emotional resonance.

29
00:02:01.783 --> 00:02:03.214
cultural soundscape.

30
00:02:03.914 --> 00:02:09.060
cultural soundscape means all the sounds that characterise a place and its culture.

31
00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:14.123
Temple bells and market calls form Phnom Penh's cultural soundscape.

32
00:02:14.823 --> 00:02:18.322
Your turn. Say: cultural soundscape.

33
00:02:21.022 --> 00:02:22.511
cultural soundscape.

34
00:02:23.517 --> 00:02:24.994
pinpeat ensemble.

35
00:02:25.694 --> 00:02:31.211
pinpeat ensemble means a traditional Khmer orchestra that accompanies court dance and ceremonies.

36
00:02:31.511 --> 00:02:34.625
The pinpeat ensemble played for the Royal Ballet.

37
00:02:35.325 --> 00:02:38.744
Your turn. Say: pinpeat ensemble.

38
00:02:41.444 --> 00:02:42.979
pinpeat ensemble.

39
00:02:43.983 --> 00:02:45.402
traditional instrument.

40
00:02:46.102 --> 00:02:51.608
traditional instrument means a musical instrument with deep roots in a culture.

41
00:02:51.908 --> 00:02:55.069
The tro is a traditional instrument played with a bow.

42
00:02:55.769 --> 00:02:59.408
Your turn. Say: traditional instrument.

43
00:03:02.108 --> 00:03:03.550
traditional instrument.

44
00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:06.654
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:06.954 --> 00:03:11.241
Rithy, why do old Khmer songs still move people so deeply?

46
00:03:12.550 --> 00:03:16.070
It might be nostalgia, but I suspect it's more than that.

47
00:03:17.383 --> 00:03:18.175
Go on.

48
00:03:19.483 --> 00:03:26.673
Many musicians were lost in the 1970s, so those recordings must feel incredibly precious.

49
00:03:27.983 --> 00:03:30.772
That would certainly explain their emotional resonance.

50
00:03:32.083 --> 00:03:36.533
And young artists may well be reviving the pinpeat ensemble in new ways.

51
00:03:37.817 --> 00:03:39.781
Could music also be political?

52
00:03:41.083 --> 00:03:46.104
It can hardly be otherwise. A protest song can achieve what a speech cannot.

53
00:03:47.417 --> 00:03:51.320
Grammar focus: Modality and nuance (advanced).

54
00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:54.839
Modality expresses fine shades of certainty.

55
00:03:55.139 --> 00:04:01.878
It might be nostalgia, but I suspect it's more than that. Those recordings must feel incredibly precious.

56
00:04:02.178 --> 00:04:05.188
Music can hardly be separated from politics.

57
00:04:05.488 --> 00:04:12.224
Combine modal verbs with phrases like 'I suspect', 'presumably' and 'can hardly' to show fine shades of certainty.

58
00:04:13.217 --> 00:04:16.391
Now read and listen. The Sound of a Nation.

59
00:04:16.691 --> 00:04:20.200
Music may be the most universal form of human expression.

60
00:04:21.517 --> 00:04:27.893
Musicology studies not only melodies but also the social meanings music carries.

61
00:04:29.183 --> 00:04:34.724
A national anthem unites citizens, while a protest song can challenge those in power.

62
00:04:36.017 --> 00:04:45.563
Cambodia's musical heritage, from the pinpeat ensemble to the golden age of 1960s pop, suffered terribly under the Khmer Rouge.

63
00:04:46.850 --> 00:04:50.510
Many musicians were killed, and countless recordings were lost.

64
00:04:51.817 --> 00:04:56.870
Surviving songs presumably owe part of their emotional resonance to this tragic history.

65
00:04:58.183 --> 00:05:04.374
Today, young musicians are reviving traditional instruments and mixing them with modern styles.

66
00:05:05.683 --> 00:05:10.934
In doing so, they are reshaping the cultural soundscape while keeping memory alive.

67
00:05:12.250 --> 00:05:14.937
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:15.237 --> 00:05:17.991
The academic study of music is blank.

69
00:05:18.291 --> 00:05:23.466
A, musicology. B, cultural soundscape. C, pinpeat ensemble.

70
00:05:27.450 --> 00:05:30.738
The answer is A: musicology.

71
00:05:32.250 --> 00:05:35.561
'Those recordings must feel precious' expresses blank.

72
00:05:35.861 --> 00:05:42.255
A, a logical deduction. B, an obligation. C, a past habit.

73
00:05:46.250 --> 00:05:49.457
The answer is A: a logical deduction.

74
00:05:50.950 --> 00:05:54.250
'It can hardly be otherwise' means it is blank.

75
00:05:54.550 --> 00:05:59.260
A, almost certainly so. B, unlikely. C, impossible to know.

76
00:06:03.250 --> 00:06:06.364
The answer is A: almost certainly so.

77
00:06:07.850 --> 00:06:10.685
A song that criticises injustice is a blank.

78
00:06:10.985 --> 00:06:17.089
A, protest song. B, national anthem. C, traditional instrument.

79
00:06:21.083 --> 00:06:23.977
The answer is A: protest song.

80
00:06:25.483 --> 00:06:28.876
According to the text, what are young musicians reviving?

81
00:06:29.176 --> 00:06:35.755
A, traditional instruments. B, only Western pop. C, national anthems.

82
00:06:39.750 --> 00:06:42.818
The answer is A: traditional instruments.

83
00:06:44.317 --> 00:06:49.500
Now it's your turn. Part one: Why should young Cambodians learn traditional music?

84
00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:59.992
Part two: Use at least three different modal expressions to show degrees of certainty.

85
00:07:06.292 --> 00:07:19.373
Traditional music might seem old-fashioned, but I suspect it gives young people a strong identity. Our ancestors must have valued it deeply. Without it, our cultural soundscape could hardly feel Khmer.

86
00:07:19.673 --> 00:07:21.533
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:22.517 --> 00:07:28.687
Great work today! You learned eight new words and modality and nuance (advanced).

88
00:07:28.987 --> 00:07:33.311
Today's value is respect for culture. Be proud of our Khmer culture.

89
00:07:33.611 --> 00:07:36.437
See you in Unit fifty-one. Goodbye!

90
00:07:36.737 --> 00:07:39.483
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

